          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Uniform spaces were invented by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Weil"} André Weil
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            for which it makes sense to speak of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformly+continuous+map"} uniformly continuous map
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+groups"} topological groups
          %p
            A
            %em uniform structure
            or
            %em uniformity
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            consists of a collection of global binary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relations"} relations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            which allow us to say when one point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            \-close” to another
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x, y) \lt \varepsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive number
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %msup
                      %mi y
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x y^{-1} \in \varepsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighborhood"} neighborhood
            of the identity.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            The definition described above is based on
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entourages"} entourages
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bourbaki"} Bourbaki
            and is still most commonly seen today. There is an equivalent definition based on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniform+covers"} uniform covers
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasiuniform+spaces"} quasiuniform spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+spaces"} gauge spaces
            (defined below) also allows for another definition. The original definition in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bourbaki"} Bourbaki
            %a{:href => "#naive_tame_topology"} can be seen
            as describing a contravariant functor from the category of finite sets to a category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filters"} filters
            of subsets.
          %h3#ent Entourage uniformities
          %p
            A
            = succeed "," do
              %strong uniform structure
            = succeed "," do
              %strong uniformity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            consists of a collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relations"} binary relations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq X \times X
            (called
            %strong entourages
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong vicinities
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ≈
                    %mi U
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_U y
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is related to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            through
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %ol
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality+relation"} equality relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta = \{(x, x): x \in X\}
              is contained in every entourage. That is,
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi U
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ≈
                        %mi U
                      %mi x
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall U,\; \forall x,\; x \approx_U x .
            %li
              For every entourage
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \circ V \subseteq U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∘
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
              is the ordinary operation of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relational composition
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi U
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi V
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi z
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ≈
                        %mi V
                      %mi y
                      %msub
                        %mo ≈
                        %mi V
                      %mi z
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ≈
                        %mi U
                      %mi z
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall U,\; \exists V,\; \forall x, y, z,\; x \approx_V y \approx_V z \;\Rightarrow x \approx_U z .
            %li
              For every entourage
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi V
                        %mi op
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^op \subseteq U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^\op
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+relation"} opposite relation
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi U
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi V
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi y
                      %msub
                        %mo ≈
                        %mi V
                      %mi x
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ≈
                        %mi U
                      %mi y
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall U,\; \exists V,\; \forall x, y,\; y \approx_V x \;\Rightarrow\; x \approx_U y .
              %p
                In light of axiom (6), it follows that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi U
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^op
                itself is an entourage.
            %li
              There exists an entourage; in light of axiom (6), it follows that the universal relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times X
              is an entourage.
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U, V
              are entourages, so is some subset of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \cap V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \cap V
              is an entourage.
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is an entourage and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq V \subseteq X \times X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is an entourage.
          %p
            A set equipped with a uniform structure is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong uniform space
          %p
            A collection of entourages satisfying (1–5) is a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base"} base
            for a uniformity; a base is precisely what generates a uniformity by taking the upward closure. A collection satisfying (1–3) is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong preuniformity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            in the statement of these axioms with a finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1 \cap \cdots \cap V_n
            of entourages to get the concept of a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subbase"} subbase
            for a uniformity. A subbase is precisely what generates a base by closing up under finite intersections and precisely what generates a uniformity by closing up under finite intersections and taking the upward closure.
          %h4#as_a_ternary_relation As a ternary relation
          %p
            A
            %strong uniform structure
            or
            %strong uniformity
            could be represented as a family of binary relations on a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            indexed by a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            of
            %strong entourages
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %strong vicinities
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/currying"} currying
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ≈
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi S
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)\approx_{(-)}(-):X \times S \times X \to \Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            is the set of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                for every entourage
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in S
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ≈
                        %mi U
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_U x
            %li
              %p
                for every entourage
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in S
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \in S
                such that for every
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in X
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi z
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \in X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ≈
                        %mi V
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_V y
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %msub
                          %mo ≈
                          %mi V
                        %mi z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \approx_V z
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %msub
                          %mo ≈
                          %mi U
                        %mi z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_U z
            %li
              %p
                For every entourage
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in S
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \in S
                such that for every
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in X
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %msub
                          %mo ≈
                          %mi V
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_V y
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %msub
                          %mo ≈
                          %mi U
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \approx_U x
            %li
              %p
                There is an entourage
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in S
                and elements
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in X
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %msub
                          %mo ≈
                          %mi U
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_U y
            %li
              %p
                If there are entourages
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in S
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \in S
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \in S
                such that for every element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in X
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %msub
                          %mo ≈
                          %mi T
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_T y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ≈
                        %mi U
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_U y
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %msub
                          %mo ≈
                          %mi V
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_V y
            %li
              %p
                If there is an entourage
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in S
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \in S
                such that for all
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in X
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %msub
                          %mo ≈
                          %mi U
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_U y
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %msub
                          %mo ≈
                          %mi V
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_V y
          %h3#ucov Covering uniformities
          %p
            An equivalent way to characterize a uniform space is by its collection of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniform+covers"} uniform covers
            %strong cover
            of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \subseteq P(X)
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_i
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1
                %strong refines
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_2
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ≺
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1 \prec C_2
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1
                is a subset of some element of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_2
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1 \wedge C_2 \coloneqq \{ A \cap B \;|\; A \in C_1, B \in C_2 \}
                ; this is also a cover.
            %li
              %p
                For
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo ≔
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi B
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isInhabited
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C[A] \coloneqq \bigcup \{ B \in C \mid \mathrm{isInhabited}(A \cap B)\}
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mo *
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^* \coloneqq \{ C[A] \;|\; A \in C\}
                \.
          %p
            We now define a
            %strong covering uniformity
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to be a collection of covers, called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong uniform covers
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is a uniform cover, there exists a uniform cover
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo ′
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo *
                        %mo ≺
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C')^* \prec C
            %li
              %p
                There exists a uniform cover; in light of axiom (4), it follows that the cover
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{X\}
                is a uniform cover.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1, C_2
                are uniform covers, so is some cover that refines
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∧
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1 \wedge C_2
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1 \wedge C_2
                is a uniform cover.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is a uniform cover and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo ≺
                        %mi C
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \prec C'
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
                is a uniform cover.
          %p
            The axioms (2–4) here roughly correspond (respectively) to the axioms (4–6) in the entourage definition. Axiom (1) takes on all of the real work; any collection of covers that satisfies it may be called a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subbase"} subbase
            (but not corresponding directly to a subbase in the previous definition), and then anything satisfying (1–3) is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base"} base
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a uniform space defined in terms of entourages, we give it a covering uniformity by declaring a cover to be uniform if it is refined by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ U[x] \;|\; x \in X\}
            for some entourage
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi y
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo ≈
                      %mi U
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U[x] \coloneqq \{ y \;|\; x \approx_U y \}
            = succeed "-sized" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %p
            Conversely, given a covering uniformity, we define a base of entourages to consist of sets of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup \{ A \times A \;|\; A \in C\}
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            a uniform cover. That is, for each cover
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ≈
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \approx_C
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ≈
                    %mi C
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_C y
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in A
            for some
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in C
          %h3#further_definitions Further definitions
          %p We give these in terms of entourages, but they could also be given directly in terms of uniform covers if desired.
          %p
            The
            %strong uniform topology
            induced by a uniformity is defined by taking the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighborhoods"} neighborhoods
            of a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to be sets of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi y
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ≈
                    %mi U
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U[x] \coloneqq \{ y \;|\; x \approx_U y \}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is an entourage. (Recall that a subset is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open
            iff it is a neighborhood of every point that it contains.) We point out that different uniformities may give rise to the same topology (just as different metrics, even uniformly inequivalent ones, may give rise to the same topology).
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+space"} regular
            and indeed
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completely+regular+space"} completely regular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            this is not necessarily true, and to reproduce classical results it is often useful or necessary to assume that uniform spaces are regular, completely regular, or satisfy an intermediate condition called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformly+regular+space"} uniform regularity
          %p
            Given uniform spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to Y
            is said to be
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformly+continuous+map"} uniformly continuous
            if for every entourage
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ×
                  %mi f
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f \times f)^{-1}(V)
            is an entourage of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous
            with respect to the corresponding uniform topologies, but the converse is false (although see below the discussion in the case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Unif
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Unif
            of uniform spaces and uniformly continuous maps.
          %p One feature of uniform space theory which is not available for general topological spaces is the possibility of taking (Cauchy) completions. The relevant definitions are straightforward:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+net"} Cauchy net
                in a uniform space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                consists of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+set"} directed set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                and a function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon D \to X
                such that for every entourage
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \in D
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo ≈
                        %mi U
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(m) \approx_U f(n)
                whenever
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %mo ,
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m, n \geq N
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong Cauchy filter
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+space"} Cauchy space
            %li
              %p
                A Cauchy net
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon D \to X
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"} converges
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon X
                if for every entourage
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \in D
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mi N
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi x
                        %msub
                          %mo ≈
                          %mi U
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq N \;\Rightarrow\; x \approx_U f(n)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nets"} nets
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filters"} filters
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence+space"} convergence space
            %li
              %p
                A uniform space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+space"} complete
                if every Cauchy net/filter in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                converges (not necessarily to a unique point).
            %li
              %p
                A uniform space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff
                or
                %strong separated
                if every convergent net/filter converges to a unique point, or equivalently if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
                whenever
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ≈
                        %mi U
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_U y
                for every entourage
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %p
            Every uniform space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            admits a Hausdorff completion
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{X}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \overline{X}
            (an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embedding"} embedding
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is Hausdorff,
            %span.newWikiWord
              dense
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/dense+map"} ?
            in any case), characterized by the following universal property:
          %ul
            %li
              Every uniformly continuous map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to Y
              to a complete Hausdorff uniform space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              extends uniquely to a uniformly continuous map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi f
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mover
                        %mi X
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{f}\colon \overline{X} \to Y
          %p
            In short, the category of complete Hausdorff uniform spaces is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Unif
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Unif
          %p
            One can also define a (not necessarily Hausdorff) completion of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            by replacing the image of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{X}
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            itself, but this does not have as nice properties; in particular, complete uniform spaces do not form a reflective subcategory of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Unif
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Unif
          %p
            Every uniform space also has an underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proximity"} proximity
            (defined there), and the resulting functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Unif
                  %mo →
                  %mi Prox
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Unif \to Prox
            has a fully faithful right adjoint which identifies proximity spaces with uniform spaces that are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+bounded+space"} totally bounded
          %p
            Uniform spaces can also be identified with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntopogenous+spaces"} syntopogenous spaces
            that are both
            %em perfect
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %em symmetric
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntopogenous+space"} syntopogenous space
          %p
            Every uniform space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inequality+relation"} inequality relation
            (an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreflexive"} irreflexive
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+relation"} symmetric relation
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo #
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x # y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ≉
                      %mi U
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mathbin{&amp;#8777;}_U y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformly+regular+space"} uniformly regular
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apartness+relation"} apartness relation
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comparison"} comparison
          %h2#the_category_of_uniform_spaces The category of uniform spaces
          %p
            Uniform spaces and uniform maps form a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+category"} topological category
          %p
            In particular,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            of uniform spaces are computed similarly to the case of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
          %p
            See Chapter 21 in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joy+of+Cats"} Joy of Cats
            for more information.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            (or more generally any pseudometric space) is a uniform space, with a base of uniformities indexed by positive numbers
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            equal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/n
            for some integer
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ≈
                    %mi ϵ
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_\epsilon y
            to mean that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x,y) \lt \epsilon
            (or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x,y) \leq \epsilon
            if you prefer). Then axiom (2) may be proved by using
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon/2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformly+regular+space"} uniformly regular
            in constructive mathematics, which may be proved by using any positive number less than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            and applying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comparison"} comparison
            law. (The other axioms are easy.) Every quasi(pseudo)metric space is a quasiuniform space in the same way. We can also generalise from metric spaces to
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+spaces"} gauge spaces
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
            is a uniform space, with a base of uniformities indexed by neighbourhoods
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            of the identity element, in two ways: left and right. (These two ways agree for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+group"} compact group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+spaces"} compact spaces
            = succeed "?)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haar+measure"} Haar measure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            is a uniform space. Define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ≈
                    %mi U
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx _U y
            to mean that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi y
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in y U
            (or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi x
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in x U
            for the other way). Then axiom (2) may be proved by invoking the continuity of multiplication; constructively, we cannot prove that every topological group is uniformly regular, although this can be proved for the classical examples of Lie groups and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+spaces"} topological vector spaces
            (TVSs).
          %p
            These are in a way the motivating examples. The theory of uniformly continuous maps was developed first for metric spaces, but it was noticed that, for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metrisable"} metrisable
            TVS, it could be described entirely in terms of the topology and the addition, which immediately generalised to non-metrisable TVSs. The theory of uniform spaces covers both of these (and their generalisations to pseudometric spaces and topological groups) at once.
          %p
            We can also form certain uniformities on function spaces. For instance, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is a uniform space and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is any set, then the set of functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            has a “uniformity of
            = succeed "\u201D;" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniform+convergence"} uniform convergence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{U} = \{ (f,g) \mid \forall x\in X, (f(x),g(x))\in U \}.
          %p
            This can be defined for any set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compactness
            properties. For instance, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            from uniform regularity of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            we seem to need a condition such as that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covert+set"} covert set
          %h2#basic_results Basic Results
          %p
            A first wave of results concerns
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axioms"} separation axioms
          %ul
            %li
              Considered as topological spaces, uniform spaces are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+space"} regular
              and (more profoundly)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completely+regular+space"} completely regular
          %p As a matter of fact, a significant theorem is that a topology (on a given set) is the uniform topology for some uniformity if and only if it is completely regular. See also the discussion below on the relation with metric and pseudometric spaces.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For a uniform space, the following separation conditions are equivalent:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
                (the topology distinguishes points),
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
                (points are closed),
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"}> Hausdorff
                ),
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_3
                (regular Hausdorff),
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mrow
                          %mn 3
                          %mfrac
                            %mn 1
                            %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{3\frac1{2}}
                (completely regular Hausdorff). Of course, this follows from the fact that it is completely regular.
            %li
              %p
                The category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Unif
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Unif
                of uniform spaces admits arbitrary small
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
                (which are preserved by the forgetful functors to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                and to
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+space"} normal
                (so that separated ones would be
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_4
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
                (with its usual topology) is not a normal space.
          %p
            A second wave of results relates uniform spaces to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudometric+spaces"} pseudometric spaces
            (like metric spaces, but dropping the separation axiom that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x, y) = 0
            implies
            = succeed "):" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
          %ul
            %li
              Every uniform space embeds uniformly in a product of pseudometric spaces. A uniform space whose uniformity admits a countable subbase is uniformly isomorphic to a pseudometric space (and hence to a metric space if the uniform space is separated, in which case the uniform space is called
              = succeed ")." do
                %strong metrisable
          %p By this result, the results mentioned above on completions of uniform spaces may be proved by appeal to similar results for (pseudo)metric spaces.
          %p We also mention the special case of compact spaces:
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+space"} compact Hausdorff space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              admits a unique uniformity whose corresponding topology is the topology of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times X
          %p
            It follows then that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y
            are uniform spaces with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            compact Hausdorff and if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to Y
            is continuous, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is uniformly continuous.
          %p
            Finally, uniform spaces and uniformly continuous maps form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Unif
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Unif
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal structure
            in which the function-space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,Y]
            is the set of uniformly continuous maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
            with the uniformity of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniform+convergence"} uniform convergence
            (mentioned above). The corresponding tensor product is called the “semi-uniform product”, and is
            %em not
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to [[X,Y],Y]
            is not generally uniformly continuous. See
            %a{:href => "#Isbell"} Isbell, Chapter III, particularly Theorem 26 p46
          %p
            On the other hand,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Unif
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Unif
            also has a different closed
            %em symmetric
            monoidal structure in which the internal-hom
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{X,Y\}
            is the space of uniformly continuous functions with the uniformity of
            %em pointwise
            convergence, i.e. the subspace uniformity induced by the product uniformity on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            %a{:href => "#Isbell"} Isbell, exercise III.7, p53
          %h2#variations Variations
          %p Some authors insist that a uniform space must be separated; this can be arranged directly in the definition (that is without reference to the uniform topology or to the concept of convergent net/filter) by adding the following axiom, a sort of converse axiom (1):
          %ul
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo ≈
                      %mi U
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx_U y
              for every entourage
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
          %p This makes the discussion of completions slightly simpler.
          %p
            If the symmetry axiom (3) in the entourage definition is dropped, then the result is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong quasiuniform space
            %em every
            topological space is quasiuniformisable. (It is rather triciker to define quasiuniform spaces in terms of covers, but technically possible using covers by pairs of sets; see
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#GS72"} Gantner and Steinlage
          %p
            A set with a preuniformity or subbase (only axioms 1-3) is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong preuniform space
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+space"} gauge space
            consists of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and a collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            of pseudometrics on
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            to be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filter"} filter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            and each positive number
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Met
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Met
            (of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
            and short maps) and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Unif
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Unif
            (of uniform spaces and uniformly continuous maps) are both
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategories"} full subcategories
            %strong quasigauge space
            consists of a set and a collection of quasipseudometrics; every quasiuniform space arises from a quasigauge space.
          %p
            In weak
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            of mathematics, the theorems above may not be provable. In particular, the theorem that every uniform space arises from a gauge space is equivalent (internal to an arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            with a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+choice"} dependent choice
            (plus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            if you don't require the uniform space to be uniformly regular). If the concept is to be applied to analysis, then it may be best to define a uniform space as a gauge space satisfying a saturation condition.
          %p
            There is also a “pointless” notion of uniform space, called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniform+locale"} uniform locale
          %h2#motivation_for_the_axioms Motivation for the axioms
          %p
            The really critical axioms are (1–3): a collection of binary relations which satisfies those three axioms is a subbase (although not every subbase takes this form), from we can generate a uniform structure straightforwardly. Indeed, (4–6) simply state that the entourages form a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filter"} filter
          %p
            We draw particular attention to axiom (2), which may be called an “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi ε
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\varepsilon}{2}
            ” principle. It generalizes a principle familiar from analysis in metric spaces, where one establishes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x, z) \lt \varepsilon
            by showing there exists
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mfrac
                    %mi ε
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x, y) \lt \frac{\varepsilon}{2}
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mfrac
                      %mi ε
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(y, z) \lt \frac{\varepsilon}{2}
          %p
            For full power in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformly+regular+space"} uniform regularity
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            there is an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon'
            such that any two points are either
            = succeed "-close" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            = succeed "-far;" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ε
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon'
            to be
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ε
                    %mo ′
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon' \lt \varepsilon
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mi ε
                      %mn 2
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\varepsilon}{2} \lt \varepsilon
          %p
            Axiom (1) is a nullary version of axiom (2); together they prove that, given any entourage
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            and any integer
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            whose
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            at any point in the composite as well.
          %p
            Altogether, these may be seen as axiomatising the notion of
            %em approximate
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \approx
            is an approximate equivalence relation, then we might expect it to be
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+relation"}> reflexive
              \:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≈
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx x
              and
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+relation"}> symmetric
              \:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≈
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx y
              iff
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ≈
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \approx x
            %li
              NOT
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+relation"} transitive
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≈
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≈
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx y \approx z
              does not necessarily mean that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≈
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \approx z
          %p
            One could stop there, but this is not a very useful notion of approximation. Instead we generalise to a
            %em family
            of approximate equivalence relations and impose the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi ε
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\varepsilon}{2}
            principle to allow them to be used. This is nearly the definition of uniform space; in particular, the axiom (1) states precisely that each entourage is reflexive. The symmetry axiom (3) in the standard definition is weaker than requiring each individual entourage to be symmetric, but that is not an essential change; every uniformity has a base consisting of its symmetric entourages. The final three axioms have already been explained as a closure condition; they force equivalent uniformities on a given set (in the sense that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+function"} identity function
            on the set is uniformly continuous either way) to be equal.
          %h2#categorical_interpretation Categorical interpretation
          %p
            The idea that a uniformity is an “approximate equivalence relation” can be made precise as follows. A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
            is the same as a category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            over the poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝟚
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2}
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Rel
                    %mo =
                    %mi 𝟚
                    %mi Mat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel = \mathbb{2} Mat
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Pro
                    %mi Rel
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pro Rel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-objects"} pro-objects
            in the hom-categories of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Rel
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filters"} filters
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prometric+space"} prometric space
          %p
            In all these cases, in order to recover the correct notion of morphism abstractly, we must consider monoids in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equipment"} equipment
            rather than merely a bicategory.
          %p
            With a uniform structure on a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            associate a contravariant functor from the category of finite sets to a category of filters of subsets as follows. Call a subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^S
            %em big
            iff there is an entourage
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x\in X
                  ^S
            provided
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo ″
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x(s'),x(s''))\in U
            for each
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi s
                    %mo ″
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s',s''\in S
            = succeed "-neighbourhoods" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_X
            from the category of finite sets to the category of filters of subsets. Axiom 1 says that there is a unique big subset in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_X(\{\bullet\})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_X(\{1,2,3\})
            is the pullback of the obvious maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
